{# DNOS BGP. netlab address families map onto DNOS afi-safi names by suffixing "-unicast", so a
   single loop covers both rather than duplicating the block per family.

   IDENTITY: router-id is only rendered when netlab is the owner of record. On a device netlab
   did not create, "load merge" would otherwise REPLACE the running BGP router-id of a box that
   is already peering -- the same overwrite class as the hostname and loopback (see
   netsim/providers/external.py and RocContLab issues #70/#72). Left unset, DNOS derives it.

   IBGP OVER LOOPBACKS ON A DEVICE NETLAB DOES NOT OWN: netlab plans neighbour addresses from its
   own loopback pool, but "initial" deliberately does not configure the loopback of such a device,
   so those addresses exist nowhere and the sessions cannot come up. "update-source" below names
   the real interface, whose address is whatever the box already had. The fix is not a template
   guard -- it is to tell netlab the truth in the topology:

       nodes:
         RNS72XC:
           loopback.ipv4: 192.0.2.5/32

   With the real loopback pinned, the planned neighbour addresses match the hardware and iBGP is
   coherent. Without it, expect sessions that configure cleanly and never establish. #}
protocols
  bgp {{ bgp.as }}
{% if netlab_manage_identity|default(True) %}
    router-id {{ bgp.router_id }}
{% endif %}
{% for af in ["ipv4","ipv6"] if bgp[af] is defined %}
    address-family {{ af }}-unicast
    !
{% endfor %}
{% for n in bgp.neighbors|default([]) %}
{%   for af in ["ipv4","ipv6"] if n[af] is defined and n[af] is not sameas true %}
    neighbor {{ n[af] }}
      remote-as {{ n.as }}
{%     if n.type == "ibgp" %}
      update-source {{ loopback.ifname }}
{%     endif %}
      address-family {{ af }}-unicast
      !
    !
{%   endfor %}
{% endfor %}
  !
!
